@meta
id: 05-17
title: Three targets, three different motions
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-counts, WORD-motions, word-e
par: 57
minutes: 8
vscode: three clicks, and a hunt for the right one
open: invoice.provider.ts

@brief
You now have twelve ways to move by word: six motions, each with a
capital, all of them countable. Having twelve is only useful if choosing
between them is fast, and choosing is what this lesson grades. Par is set
to the cheapest route, not a comfortable one.

`issue` needs to take the customer's locale and pass it to the renderer,
and the subtotal reference is stale. When you are done:

  - the signature is `issue(invoice: Invoice, customer: Customer, locale: string)`
  - the total is built from `invoice.subtotalNet`
  - the render payload is `{ locale, total }`

Three insertion points, one per line. Before you move, decide for each
one: which end of the line is closer, do you want a start or an end, and
is the punctuation on the way worth counting?

@keys
| `w` `b` `e` `ge` | by word |
| `W` `B` `E` `gE` | by WORD, whitespace only |
| `3w` `2e` … | any of them, counted |
| `0` `$` | the ends of the line |

@hints
The parameter goes in front of the closing paren, which is one word back
from the end of that line. The payload key goes in front of `total`,
which is a long way from the start of its line if you count dots and no
distance at all if you do not.
--
Line by line: `$ge` then `i` for the signature; a counted `e` from column
zero for `subtotalNet`; a counted `W` for the payload.
--
`j$ge` `i, locale: string` `Esc`. Then `j0` `6e` `aNet` `Esc`. Then
`2j0` `5W` `i` `locale, ` `Esc` `:wq`.

@solution
    j $ ge   i, locale: string<Esc>
    j 0 6e   aNet<Esc>
    2j 0 5W  ilocale, <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Three targets, three different answers, and none of them was `w` pressed
repeatedly.

The first is a start-of-word target sitting behind punctuation at the end
of a line, so `$ge` — two keys, no counting. The second is an end-of-word
target near the start of its line, so a counted `e` going forwards. The
third is deep inside an argument list full of dots and parens, which is
what `W` is for: five stops instead of the nineteen `w` would have taken.

If you want one rule out of this chapter, it is this order of questions.
Which end of the line is nearer? Do I want the front or the back of the
target? Is there punctuation in between that I care about? Three answers,
and the motion picks itself.

The counting will fade. After a few weeks you stop deriving `5W` and
start seeing it, in the same way you stopped deriving `5j` from the
relative line numbers and just read it off the gutter.

@start invoice.provider.ts
export class InvoiceProvider {
  public async issue(invoice: Invoice, customer: Customer) {
    const total = invoice.subtotal + invoice.tax;

    await this.pdfService.render(invoice.id, customer.email, { total });

    return total;
  }
}

@expected invoice.provider.ts
export class InvoiceProvider {
  public async issue(invoice: Invoice, customer: Customer, locale: string) {
    const total = invoice.subtotalNet + invoice.tax;

    await this.pdfService.render(invoice.id, customer.email, { locale, total });

    return total;
  }
}
